to min but less than max is inserted, replacing the max. Our heap: root dist 5, d=3 < 5 so replace root -> selected {idx0,new}. Good.\n\nWhat about new point d=3 equal to both? List [3(idx0),3(idx1)] full; sequential: x=0: 3<3 false; x=1: 3<3 false; no insertion. Our heap: root dist 3 (with larger index among the two, say idx1). New point d=3 equal to root.dist and p maybe less than root.idx? If new point has smaller index than root's index, our condition would replace root because d == root.dist && p < root.idx. But sequential algorithm does NOT insert on equal distance when list is full, regardless of index. So this would be a mismatch! Let's test: points order: idx0 d=3, idx1 d=5, idx2 d=3? Actually we need scenario where after filling k with two points both having same max distance and new point has same distance but earlier index than the one that is root (which might have larger index). Example: k=2. Points:\n- p0 d=3\n- p1 d=5\n- p2 d=3\n\nSequential:\nProcess p0 -> [3]\np1 -> insert at pos1 because empty? Actually after p0, array [-1,-1]? Wait initial distances -1. Process p0: x=0: 3 < -1 false but distances[0]==-1 true -> insert at pos0 => [3,-1].\nProcess p1 d=5: x=0: 5<3 false; x=1: distances[1]==-1 true -> insert at pos1 => [3,5].\nNow list full.\nProcess p2 d=3: x=0: 3<3 false; x=1: 3<5 true -> shift right (none) and set distances[1]=3 => [3,3]. So selected {p0,p2}. Our heap:\n- Insert p0 (size1)\n- Insert p1 (size2): root is worst among {p0(3),p1(5)} -> p1.\n- Process p2 d=3 < root.dist 5 -> replace root with p2. Selected {p0,p2}. Good.\n\nNow consider scenario where after filling, the two selected have equal max distance and new point has same distance but earlier index than one of them? Example: k=2. Points:\n- p0 d=4\n- p1 d=3\n- p2 d=4\n\nSequential:\np0 -> [4]\np1 -> insert at pos0 because 3<4 => [3,4] (after shift)\nNow list full with distances [3(p1),4(p0)].\np2 d=4: x=0: 4<3 false; x=1: 4<4 false (strict) -> no insertion. Selected {p1,p0}.\nOur