\nelse if (d < heap_dist[0]) replace root and sift down;\n```\n\nWe don't need the tie-break by index at all! Because new points always have larger indices, so equal distance never triggers replacement. This is a key insight.\n\nThus we can simplify: only strict less than root when full.\n\nLet's verify with earlier example where sequential inserted on equal distance because there was an empty slot? That happens only during filling phase; our heap also inserts then.\n\nSo the selection algorithm becomes:\n- Maintain max-heap of size k (or up to total_points if fewer).\n- For each point in order:\n  - If heap_size < k: insert.\n  - Else if d < root.dist: replace root and sift down.\n\nThis exactly matches sequential behavior because once full, only strictly smaller distances are inserted; equal or larger are ignored. And since we process points in original order, the set of selected indices will be identical to reference.\n\nGreat! So our heap implementation can drop the tie-break condition.\n\nNow let's adjust code accordingly: remove `|| (d == heap_dist[0] && p < heap_idx[0])`.\n\nAlso note that when replacing root, we don't need index comparison; just replace if d < root.dist.\n\nThus selection loop:\n```\nfor (int p = 0; p < total_points; p++) {\n    float d = dists[p];\n    if (heap_size < k) {\n        heap_dist[heap_size] = d;\n        heap_idx[heap_size] = p;\n        heap_size++;\n        sift_up(heap_dist, heap_idx, heap_size - 1);\n    } else if (d < heap_dist[0]) {\n        heap_dist[0] = d;\n        heap_idx[0] = p;\n        sift_down(heap_dist, heap_idx, heap_size, 0);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is simpler and correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure that `worse` function still works for max-heap: root should be the largest distance. Since all distances are non-negative (squared), fine.\n\nWe also need to handle case where total_points < k; then heap_size = total_points, and frequency counting over heap_size is correct.\n\nNow let's finalize code with proper includes and alignment.\n\nPotential issue: `posix_memalign` may not be declared if we don't define feature macros. We'll add at top of knn.c:\n```\n#define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L\n#include \"knn.h\"\n#ifdef __ARM_NEON\n#include <arm_neon.h>\n#endif\n```\n\nAlso, in NEON loop, we used `vld2q_f32` which requires the pointer to be aligned? It should work with unaligned loads; but for performance alignment helps. We allocated dists aligned 16, but g.points may not be aligned. That's fine.\n\nWe also need to ensure that `float32x4_t` is available; include arm_neon.h only if __ARM_NEON defined (which it will on ARM64 with NEON). On other architectures, fallback scalar loop.\n\nNow let's write the final content for knn.c. We'll keep knn.h unchanged.\n\nBut we must also consider that `euclidean_distance_no_sqrt` is in knn.h and used nowhere now; but we can leave it.\n\nLet's produce the JSON response with files array containing src/knn.c (and maybe src/knn.h if changed? We won't change knn.h). So only one file.\n\nWe need to include complete content of changed files. Since we only modify knn.c, we'll provide that.\n\nNow let's craft the final answer.\n\nHypothesis: \"Using ARM NEON intrinsics to compute squared distances for four points at a time and selecting top-k with a max-heap (strictly replacing on smaller distance) preserves exact tie-breaking while improving SIMD utilization.\"\n\nTransformations list:\n- Restructured knn() to first compute all squared distances into an aligned array using NEON vectorized loads/stores.\n- Replaced insertion-sort selection with a max-heap of size k, processing points in original order and replacing root only when distance is strictly smaller (preserving tie-breaking).\n- Used posix_memalign for 16-byte alignment to support NEON operations.\n\nSources_kept: [] since integration_source_ids empty.\n\nNotes: mention that we rely on ARM64 NEON; fallback scalar path included via #ifdef.\n\nNow produce JSON."
